``
If API latency spikes immediately after a deployment, the first goal is to determine whether the deployment caused it or merely coincided with another issue. I'd work through the highest-signal checks first:

Confirm the deployment is the trigger
Compare latency graphs immediately before and after deployment.
Check whether all endpoints are affected or only specific ones.
Verify if error rates and request volume also changed.
Look at application metrics
p50, p95, and p99 latency
Request throughput (RPS)
Error rates (4xx/5xx)
CPU, memory, and thread utilization
Garbage collection pauses (for JVM/.NET applications)
Compare the new version with the previous one
Review the deployment diff.
Check for:
New database queries
Additional API calls
New middleware
Feature flags that became enabled
Logging changes (excessive synchronous logging can increase latency)
Check database performance
Slow query logs
Query execution plans
Lock contention
Connection pool saturation
Missing indexes introduced by new queries

Inspect downstream dependencies

External APIs
Cache (Redis/Memcached)
Message queues
Authentication providers

Often the application is waiting on another service rather than spending CPU time itself.

Review infrastructure health
Pod/container restarts
CPU throttling
Memory pressure
Autoscaling events
Load balancer health
Network latency between services
Check traces (distributed tracing)
If using tracing (e.g., OpenTelemetry, Jaeger, Zipkin), identify:
Which span suddenly became slower.
Whether time is spent in the application, database, or external service.
Verify configuration changes
Sometimes the code isn't the problem. Check for:
Environment variables
Database connection pool size
Cache configuration
Timeouts
Feature flags
Resource limits (CPU/memory)
Look for deployment-specific issues
Cold caches
JIT compilation or warm-up
Missing migrations
Traffic imbalance across instances
Canary vs. full rollout behavior

Decide whether to roll back
If:

latency increased immediately after deployment,
the increase is significant,
and the root cause isn't quickly identified,

a rollback is often the fastest way to restore service while investigation continues.
